 authors and and  organize it but I do think that that  people are more receptive to this idea I  think people have thought a lot about  courage in the past since 9/11 I think  it's really changed our view of public  service and I think people really  recognize the sacrifices that public  servants often make and how indebted we  are and I think it's really inspired  people to give more of themselves to  their community so in that way I think  that it that it's it's changed it but  but as I said I think this is something  that is always there and it's always  important and we need to think about  this is a question I think for Caroline  but really for anybody and it simply  says would LBJ signing of the 1964 civil  rights bill qualify as a Profile in  Courage I see heads nodding but I'm not  hearing him yeah okay  well I think it was they act the  terrorists for President Lyndon Johnson  to respond to what was happening in  America we had witnessed the march on  Washington in 1963 President Kennedy had  made an unbelievable wonderful speech in  June of 1963 talking about the question  of civil rights the question of a raised  as a moral issue there was a sense of a  national crisis in American society and  both President Kennedy and later  President Johnson with the Congress  responded I think for President Lyndon  Johnson did in 1964 and later in 1965  and sign in is the right side I think it  must be looked upon as Profile in  Courage it this was a man who came from  Texas a southerner and he had to  convince some people to be quiet not to  make so much noise and convince other  people to vote for the legislation is  six the forces of right side and it's 65  Voting Rights Act I don't know if the 64  Civil Rights Act had passed or the 65  Voting Rights Act had passed I don't  know what would have happened to our  country we could have ended up something  like South Africa Northern Ireland I  don't know but I think it must be looked  up upon as an I the courage it wa